export default "## Why Blood Color Matters\n\nMenstrual blood color changes naturally across a period and across cycles. The color you see depends on how long the blood has been in the uterus, how fast it is flowing, and how much it mixes with cervical mucus on its way out. Pink blood most often means diluted blood. It is light flow mixed with discharge. But it can also signal hormonal patterns worth paying attention to.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only. It is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## When Pink Blood Is Normal\n\n### Start and End of Your Period\n\nThe first day or two of a period, flow is often light. Menstrual blood at low volumes mixes with cervical fluid and appears pink or light red. As flow increases, the color deepens to a fuller red. The final days of a period often produce light pink or brownish discharge as the remaining blood trickles out slowly.\n\nPink blood that transitions in and out of normal red flow is part of the natural range of menstrual variation.\n\n### After Exercise\n\nVigorous exercise can temporarily increase vaginal discharge and affect how menstrual blood appears. Some people notice lighter, pinker flow during or after intense workouts. This is more common when you are already at the lighter end of your period.\n\n### Ovulation Spotting\n\nMid-cycle spotting around the time of ovulation is sometimes pink. It can last a day or two. This comes from the brief hormonal shift around the LH surge and is generally harmless. It can be surprising if you are not expecting it.\n\n## When Pink Blood May Signal Something Worth Tracking\n\n### Low Estrogen\n\nEstrogen builds the uterine lining during the first half of the cycle. When estrogen is low, the lining may be thinner than usual. A thin lining sheds less blood. That blood appears pink or very light red throughout the period rather than red.\n\nLow estrogen can result from high athletic training intensity, low body weight, perimenopause, or certain medical conditions. If your periods have become progressively lighter and pinker over several cycles, that pattern is worth noting and discussing with a provider.\n\n### Hormonal Contraceptives\n\nLow-dose hormonal contraceptives deliberately thin the uterine lining. Many people on hormonal pills, patches, or IUDs experience lighter, shorter periods with pinkish rather than red flow. If you recently started or adjusted a hormonal contraceptive, this change in color is likely method-related.\n\n### Possible Implantation Bleeding\n\nImplantation bleeding occurs about 6 to 12 days after ovulation if a fertilized egg has attached to the uterine lining. It is typically pink or brown and very light. If pink spotting appears around your expected period date but is unusually light and brief, a pregnancy test after the expected period date is the most direct way to investigate.\n\n### Cervical Sensitivity or Ectropion\n\nPink spotting after sex, after a pelvic exam, or after vigorous activity can come from the cervix rather than the uterus. Cervical ectropion, where fragile cells from the cervical canal are exposed on the outer cervix, is common and usually harmless. It often produces light pink spotting from minor friction.\n\n### Perimenopause\n\nAs ovarian function changes in the years before menopause, estrogen levels become less predictable. Lighter, pinker periods are common during perimenopause, alongside irregular cycle lengths and other changes.\n\n## What to Log\n\nTracking blood color gives you and your healthcare provider more to work with than cycle dates alone. When you notice pink blood, log:\n\n- **Which day of your period it occurred**, first day, middle, or end\n- **Flow volume**, spotting only, light, or your usual flow volume but pink\n- **Duration**, did it transition to red, or was the entire period pink\n- **Where you are in your cycle**, mid-cycle, premenstrual, or expected period time\n- **Recent changes**, new contraceptive, recent illness, significant stress, major weight change\n- **Associated symptoms**, cramping, unusual discharge, pain, or odor\n\nA few cycles of this data creates a useful baseline. New patterns that persist across cycles are what warrant provider attention.\n\n## When to See a Healthcare Provider\n\nContact a provider if you notice:\n\n- Pink-only flow across multiple consecutive cycles with no obvious cause\n- Pink or light brown spotting at mid-cycle that is new and recurring\n- Pink spotting after sex that recurs more than once or twice\n- Pink blood accompanied by pelvic pain, unusual odor, or abnormal discharge\n- Any unusual bleeding if you are postmenopausal\n\n## A Note on Period Blood Color Data and Privacy\n\nLogging period blood color, flow, and associated symptoms creates a detailed picture of your reproductive health. This is genuinely useful health information. It is also sensitive.\n\nFlo Health was the subject of an FTC enforcement action in 2021 for sharing period and health data with Facebook and Google. A $59.5 million class action settlement followed in 2025. Period tracking data is not covered by HIPAA. In states where abortion is criminalized, this data can be subpoenaed from cloud-based apps.\n\nOn-device trackers like Floriva, Drip, and Euki store nothing on external servers. Your logged blood colors, flow notes, and symptom observations stay on your device. There is no readable central record to be subpoenaed or breached.";
